; Listing text with one bad line per defect, each beside its corrected form.
; Every bad line is reported and no good line is. This run is not published
; (a blank line follows it), so nothing here is a prose unit.
;
; Bad, and the defect each one shows:
;   $D010  a one-byte variable described at length, listing its writers
;   $D011  a variable naming the routine that writes it
;   $F601  a data row opening on its index, with a tile code beside a name
;   $F603  a data row opening on a code range
;   $F605  a noun-phrase row ending in a full stop
;   $F607  an entry title counting its contents
;   $F610  a title holding a constant's identifier and a hyphen dash
;   $F620  a data entry pointing at "the routines below"
;   $F630  a data entry describing another routine's logic
;   $F640  an argument against a reading nobody offered
;   $F650  the same, as "X, not Y"
;   $F660  the same, as "Instead of"
;   $F670  a DEFW row whose comment is its operand label's words
;   $F680  a note written with "b" for the flat
;   $F690  ASCII stand-ins for ×, →, ° and the en dash
;   $F6A0  a render inside a description sentence
;   $F6B0  an unused entry saying "Unused."
;
; Good, at the address after each bad one, or in the entry beside it: the same
; content with the defect removed.
; Two more defects are not here: one-byte rows of a multi-byte record and an
; unused span under a used label, which the data-structure and identity checks
; report, not this linter. A plain word in the glossary needs a reader and is
; not here either.
;

; Enemy hit-type code
;
; Which path removes a struck enemy.
@label=removal_code
g$D010 DEFB $00      ; Enemy hit-type code read by #R$A010 to pick the removal
                     ; path. 0 = a shot hit, set by the shot collision test
                     ; #R$A020. A heavy shot such as the LASER bolt
                     ; force-clears at #R$A030, and a plain shot checks armor
                     ; at #R$A040. 1 = an ordinary enemy step, the default
                     ; #R$A050 sets. It checks armor at #R$A040 and spares the
                     ; armored robot. 2 = the bomb step, set by #R$A050 for the
                     ; bomb only. That step takes the force-clear #R$A030,
                     ; which empties any enemy slot, the armored robot too. The
                     ; only routines that write it are #R$A020 and #R$A050
                     ; (#R$A050 setting 1 or 2).

; Heading code for the player
@label=player_heading
g$D011 DEFB $00      ; Heading code for the player. Each active sprite's code
                     ; comes from the #R$A060 table, written by #R$A070.

; Enemy hit type
;
; Which path removes a struck enemy.
@label=hit_type
g$D012 DEFB $00      ; 0 a shot, 1 an enemy, 2 the bomb

; Heading code
@label=heading
g$D013 DEFB $00      ; Heading code of the player

; Enemy attribute table
;
; One attribute byte per enemy design, indexed by type code.
@label=enemy_attr
b$F601 DEFB $45      ; Code $1C: ROBOT enemy (top-left tile $5A), bright blue
 $F602 DEFB $45      ; ROBOT enemy, bright blue
 $F603 DEFB $46      ; Codes $70-$78: GHOST enemies
 $F604 DEFB $46      ; GHOST enemies
 $F605 DEFB $47      ; Armored-1 enemy.
 $F606 DEFB $47      ; Armored-1 enemy

; The 50 records
@label=level_records
b$F607 DEFB $00

; Level by level
@label=level_cards
b$F608 DEFB $00

; Work buffer (WORK_BUF) - redraw flags
@label=work_buffer
s$F610 DEFS $10

; Work buffer
@label=work_buffer_good
s$F620 DEFS $01

; Redraw flags
;
; Why the address: the routines below derive this base…
@label=redraw_flags
s$F621 DEFS $01

; Redraw flags, one per screen cell
;
; Filled by #R$A200.
@label=redraw_flags_good
s$F622 DEFS $01

; Back buffer
;
; The page is also used as a bit field. The address routine #R$A311 packs the
; screen third and the cell's pixel row into the low bits of the page, and the
; sprite copy routine #R$A3FC masks the third back out to find the cell's
; redraw flag.
@label=back_buffer
s$F630 DEFS $01

; Back buffer, one byte per screen byte
;
; Filled by #R$A3FC.
@label=back_buffer_good
s$F631 DEFS $01

; End marker
;
; One byte is not a flag: the final byte of the grid holds the $FF end
; marker.
@label=end_marker
g$F640 DEFB $FF

; End marker
;
; The final byte of the grid holds the $FF end marker.
@label=end_marker_good
g$F641 DEFB $FF

; Enemy contact test
;
; Loops over every sprite but the player's shield. Those skips belong to the
; loop, not to the caller, so an enemy that never runs the shot test #R$A080
; still gets them.
@label=contact_test
c$F650 RET

; Enemy contact test
;
; Loops over every sprite but the player's shield. An enemy that never runs
; the shot test #R$A080 still gets these skips.
@label=contact_test_good
c$F651 RET

; Ghost
;
; Frame handler for the ghost. Instead of wandering it homes on
; the player.
@label=ghost
c$F660 RET

; Ghost
;
; Frame handler for the ghost. It homes on the player.
@label=ghost_good
c$F661 RET

; Handler table
;
; One handler per enemy.
@label=handlers
w$F670 DEFW $A14B     ; drone
 $F672 DEFW $A14B     ; falls toward the player

; Note period/duration table
;
; The 36 pitches the tune player can play, two bytes each.
@label=tone_table
b$F680 DEFB $40,$01   ; Eb4
 $F682 DEFB $3C,$01   ; E♭4

; Room grid
;
; A 2x2-character block, a 16x16-pixel tile.
; .
; The grid is 8 x 6 of them, 128 x 96 pixels.
; .
; The cell address is row * 32 + col.
; .
; A cell's tile -> its attribute byte.
; .
; The diagonal headings sit 45 deg apart.
; .
; Rows 2-17 hold the grid.
@label=grid
b$F690 DEFB $00

; Room grid
;
; A 2×2-character block, a 16×16-pixel tile.
; .
; The grid is 8×6 of them, 128×96 pixels.
; .
; The cell address is row × 32 + col.
; .
; A cell's tile → its attribute byte.
; .
; The diagonal headings sit 45° apart.
; .
; Rows 2–17 hold the grid.
@label=grid_good
b$F691 DEFB $00

; Player sprite
;
; The player #UDGARRAY2,7,4,2($B801,,2;$B800,,2)(player_img) in its plain form,
; drawn at the starting point.
@label=player_sprite
b$F6A0 DEFB $00,$00

; Player sprite
;
; The player in its plain form, drawn at the starting point.
; .
; #UDGTABLE { #UDGARRAY2,7,4,2($B801,,2;$B800,,2)(player_img_good) } TABLE#
@label=player_sprite_good
b$F6A2 DEFB $00,$00

; Unused bytes
;
; Unused.
@label=unused_f6b0
u$F6B0 DEFS $06

; Unused bytes
@label=unused_f6b6
u$F6B6 DEFS $06

@label=update_drone
c$A14B RET

@label=decode_heading
c$A200 RET

@label=address_builder
c$A311 RET

@label=sprite_copy
c$A3FC RET
